  "method": "Third pass. Every check in this pass uses native tools only, following AGENTS.md 'Implementation language: no Python'. Hashes: both binaries' sha256 (sha256sum) equal aa2_20261029/runs/arms.sha256, and each file's BLAKE3 (upstream C reference implementation, blake3 1.8.7 portable build, compiled with gcc) equals software.binary_blake3 in every recorded and replayed output of its arm. Replays (unchanged from the first pass): each binary ran three times, alternating baseline and candidate, one process at a time, with RAYON_NUM_THREADS=1, on 'ic price --params /home/user/crypto/research/ic_tool_program/rule/v3/runs/k0n53/T01.params.json --json --out <scratch>'. Comparison: every recorded output (126) and every replay (6) was reduced with the jq 1.7 filter in strip_filter_jq, which deletes exactly keys_dropped and fails if a repetition keeps any other key. The result was canonicalised with 'jq -S -c' and hashed with sha256sum. All 132 give one hash, in both arms, so every remaining key is identical: counters, relation counts, status, all_verified, recovered and the params path. Scalars: verify_scalars.rs, a single Rust file with no dependencies and no repository code, compiled with 'rustc -O' and run on all 132 outputs. It builds GF(2^53) = GF(2)[x]/(x^53+x^6+x^2+x+1) and checks the modulus irreducible (Rabin). On y^2+xy = x^3+1 it checks that #E = 9007199311360364 and the trace -56619371 from the Frobenius trace recurrence equal the registry, that r = 21044858204113 is prime (deterministic Miller-Rabin) with r*428 = #E, that G is on the curve with r*G = O, and that a Frobenius eigenvalue lambda has Frobenius(G) = lambda*G. It derives Q from public_hash_seed 23001 with its own BLAKE3 (self-tested on the published empty and 'abc' digests) by the workflow's hash-to-curve, and checks that Q equals the recorded Q, lies on the curve and has r*Q = O. It then checks d*G == Q for the single d = 518509155375 that all 132 outputs report, with negative controls: (d+1)*G != Q, seed+1 gives another point on E, and an end-to-end tampered input (recovered d+1) makes the verifier exit 1. verify_scalars.py and the Python comparison script of the first pass are superseded and kept only as historical evidence.",
